    "message": "feat(request-log): store bodies zstd-compressed in a side table\n\nBodies are 99% of the stored bytes on a real database -- 30.7 KB of\nrequest and response text against 332 bytes of metadata -- so retention\nis really a disk decision. Two things follow, and this does both.\n\nThey move to their own table, which SQLite's own guidance recommends: a\nrow larger than a page spills into a chain of overflow pages that every\ntable scan then walks, and any update rewrites the whole cell.\n\nAnd they compress far better than they look, because consecutive\nrequests repeat a near-identical system prompt and conversation history\n-- redundancy per-row compression cannot see. A dictionary trained on\nthe traffic itself takes 2.78x to 8.2x on a held-out split. zstd is in\nthe 3.14 standard library, so this adds no dependency.\n\nReplaying 4,000 real requests through both write paths: 168.5 MB inline\nagainst 28.2 MB compressed, 6.0x smaller for byte-identical content\n(41.1 -> 6.9 KB a row), every row verified present and round-tripping.\nReading one back costs ~24us.\n\n- Blobs record which dictionary compressed them, so retraining can never\n  orphan an older row. Training happens once a few hundred bodies exist,\n  during the first session rather than after a restart.\n- Nothing migrates. Rows written by an older version keep their inline\n  text and are read from there until retention drains them; search spans\n  both forms via a SQL function over the compressed payload, which costs\n  no storage where an FTS index would give back much of the saving.\n- prune and clear own the child table, or blobs outlive their rows and\n  the file grows forever.\n- REQUEST_LOG_COMPRESS_BODIES=false restores the old inline behaviour.\n\nAlso fixes silent data loss found while measuring this: close() waited a\nfixed 5s for the writer, and compressing a deep queue takes longer than\nthat, so 2,950 of the first 4,000 replayed rows were dropped without a\nword. The wait now scales with the backlog and reports anything it\ngenuinely abandons.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
